WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:02.750
SPEAKER 1: As a developer,
I often find myself

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:05.720
needing to store data in
a relational database.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.520
I'd really like to
let someone else worry

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.920
about the management of
the database architecture,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.580
and I'd like it to scale easily
without worsening performance.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.890
I know Cloud Spanner is a
great solution for scalability,

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.280
but I don't have a lot of
data, and my application

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.250
generates a fairly low amount
of requests at the moment.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.370
I've heard that Spanner is only
for really large use cases,

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.320
like storing YouTube data.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.740
I'm concerned that Spanner may
be too much for my workload.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.410
If this sounds like you, the
Google Cloud Spanner team

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.450
is happy to introduce
granular instance sizing.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.740
Now you can use Spanner from
a much smaller starting point

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.200
and at a lower cost.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.200
Cloud Spanner is a great
choice for any application that

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.680
requires availability and strong
consistency without losing out

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:49.790
on performance.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.770
As a managed Cloud
native database,

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.780
Spanner provides
high availability

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.680
by managing additional copies
of your data, called replicas.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.990
Replicas ensure
data is persisted

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.660
and requests will continue
to work seamlessly

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.209
without downtime and
without the need for you

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:07.820
to manage complicated
architectures.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.760
As a horizontally scaling
relational database,

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.450
Spanner is able to guarantee
strong consistency of your data

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:18.470
by utilizing ACID transactions
and leveraging a distributed

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.690
clock called
TrueTime to guarantee

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:25.280
ordering of commits even
across geographic locations.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.310
This consistency is critical
to prevent unexpected data

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.720
discrepancies.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.910
As one example,
imagine two players

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.950
of a game making a trade.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.050
Consistency is
needed to guarantee

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:39.410
the first player gets the money
and the second gets the item.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.170
Spanner also allows
you to easily scale up

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.480
as your workload grows
without requiring

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.790
you to manage complex sharding.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.650
One way Spanner
is able to support

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.280
the scalability is by
separating compute and storage.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.190
When you create a Spanner
instance, up until now

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.610
you specify the instance
size in terms of nodes.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:02.720
Nodes determine how much compute
and storage you have access to.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.550
It does not impact
the availability

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.370
guarantees of Spanner or
the number of replicas

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.020
your instance has.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.480
A single node provides
enough compute capacity

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.290
for up to 10,000 reads
per second or up to 2,000

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:16.670
writes per second.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.510
You also get storage capacity
for 4 terabytes of data.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.280
This compute and
storage capacity

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.710
might be too much for
many applications,

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.860
and this overprovisioning may
have caused application teams

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.010
to hesitate to choose Spanner
despite the great benefits

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.900
of the database solution.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.120
With the release of
granular instance sizing,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.360
it is now easier than ever and
more cost-efficient to start

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:39.770
using Spanner.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:42.470
Granular instance sizing
introduces a new unit

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.150
called processing units.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:48.530
1,000 processing units is the
equivalent of a single node.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:50.450
When you create a
new instance, you

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.630
can either specify the
size as a number of nodes

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.060
or a number of processing units.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.750
You can size your instance as
low as 100 processing units,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.620
or 1/10 the storage and compute
capacity of a single node,

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.690
at 1/10 the cost.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.450
Granular instance sizing lets
you get started with Spanner

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.330
starting at $65 a month.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:14.570
For example, now your team can
get started with test and dev

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.360
instances that don't need
the full compute capacity

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.340
a single node provides.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.220
You can size the instances for
the amount of compute capacity

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.270
you need.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.280
If your capacity
needs start to grow,

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.230
Spanner makes it
easy to scale up

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.930
without downtime to your users.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:31.610
Scaling up can be
done incrementally

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.640
to avoid overprovisioning
your Spanner resources.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.710
Granular instance
sizing allows your team

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.380
to get the incredible
features of Spanner, including

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.770
strong consistency, high
availability, and scalability,

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:46.050
without overpaying
for unused resources.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.590
So go ahead and provision
your Spanner instance

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.150
with granular instance sizing.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:53.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

